UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 1, Lesson 1: Science in everyday life', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence. A scientific claim is supported by observations or data, not only personal belief. Distinguish curiosity questions from questions that can be investigated with measurements or controlled comparisons.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite ''Why is science cool?'' into one everyday phenomenon plus one testable question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nList five everyday phenomena; write a testable question for three; for one, outline what evidence you would collect (school-safe).\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science","learning_intention":"Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence. A scientific claim is supported by observations or data, not only personal belief. Distinguish curiosity questions from questions that can be investigated with measurements or controlled comparisons.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["SC4-WS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: List five everyday phenomena; write a testable question for three; for one, outline what evidence you would collect (school-safe). Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about science question linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Treating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence.. Correction: Strong answer: Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared. with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses science question. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about science question, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about science question. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for science question. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for science question. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared","Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Rewrite ''Why is science cool?'' into one everyday phenomenon plus one testable question. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for science question; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared","Careful use of evidence and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with science question?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named evidence and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","Name evidence and explain why it matters only, without linking it to science question","Apply science question to new material and name the evidence and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies science question with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation"],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which claim about science question is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence","Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","A named observation plus how it supports the claim"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about science question?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about science question with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared","Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Using this science idea — Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence. — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Identify the independent variable, control the rest, and record repeatable measurements","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about science question?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about science question should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true","Treating opinions (''I reckon'') as conclusions without evidence"],"question":"Which prediction about science question can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about science question. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses science question. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about science question linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for science question. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about science question, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Treating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence.. Correction: Strong answer: Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared. with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-sci-y7-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise science question.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Working Scientifically","success_criteria":["I can use science question with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify evidence and explain why it matters.","I can practise everyday phenomenon independently on a new prompt.","I can notice claim needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence.","options":["Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about science question with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPhenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type change how quickly temperature readings fall when both start at the same temperature? Claim later must cite thermometer data.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite ''Why is science cool?'' into one everyday phenomenon plus one testable question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared.","explain":"Science explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence. A scientific claim is supported by observations or data, not only personal belief. Distinguish curiosity questions from questions that can be investigated with measurements or controlled comparisons.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Science around us.","caption":"Evidence later.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phenomenon</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">everyday event</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Testable Q</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">can measure</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Phenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type chang Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Phenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type chang Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about science question with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPhenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type change how quickly temperature readings fall when both start at the same temperature? Claim later must cite thermometer data. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Claims vs opinions.","caption":"Support with evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Claim</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">needs data</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Opinion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">not enough</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about science question with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTreating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence. Here is a more productive model: Phenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type change how quickly temperature readings fall when both start at the same temperature? Claim later must cite thermometer data.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Names a concrete phenomenon and a question that could be measured or fairly compared.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Treating opinions (''I reckon…'') as conclusions without evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about science question with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nScience explains everyday phenomena with testable questions and evidence. A scientific claim is supported by observations or data, not only personal belief. Distinguish curiosity questions from questions that can be investigated with measurements or controlled comparisons. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 1, Lesson 1: Science in everyday life","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Working scientifically and lab safety","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nPhenomenon: a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon at room temperature. Testable angle: Does material type change how quickly temperature readings fall when both start at the same temperature? Claim later must cite thermometer data.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Science around us.","caption":"Evidence later.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phenomenon</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">everyday event</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Testable Q</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">can measure</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Claims vs opinions.","caption":"Support with evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Claim</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">needs data</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Opinion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">not enough</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s4-sci-y7-w01-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
